I tried this code:

```Rust
fn main() {
    let tests = [
        "saddasadadsadafdsdafhsdfjhsdkjgfhfskdgvhsdjgfhsjagsjagduagfjhsdagfysdagfusdfhsds",
        ".",
        "skdghsdhjajgdsdasdsafdsdaffdsfdsfhgasdgfhdsa.afdjhasdhjghsajdhasgdhjasd",
        "\u{ad}\u{ad}.\u{ad}\u{ad}",
        "",
    ];

    for test in tests {
        println!("{test:?}");

        let timer = std::time::Instant::now();
        for _ in 0..1_000_000 {
            slow(std::hint::black_box(test));
        }
        println!("  Slow: {:?}", timer.elapsed());

        let timer = std::time::Instant::now();
        for _ in 0..1_000_000 {
            fast(std::hint::black_box(test));
        }
        println!("  Fast: {:?}", timer.elapsed());

        println!();
    }

}

fn slow(x: &str) -> bool {
    x.contains('.')
}

fn fast(x: &str) -> bool {
    x.as_bytes().iter().any(|&b| b == b'.')
}
```

I expected `slow` to be about as fast as `fast`. Maybe a bit slower for small inputs due to prioritizing making large inputs faster. Instead it's dramatically worse. On my machine this outputs:

